linuxfw: relocate fwmark bits to avoid Android netd

In Android, netd owns the low 21 bits of the packet mark and bits
29:31, so upstream's bits 16:23 collide with netd's netId/flag
fields and short-circuit its per-network/per-uid policy. Move the
Tailscale bits to 25:28, the upper half of netd's reserved byte.

diff --git a/tsconst/linuxfw.go b/tsconst/linuxfw.go
index 3a7a4cf..a0f1033 100644
--- a/tsconst/linuxfw.go
+++ b/tsconst/linuxfw.go
@@ -7,37 +7,39 @@
 
 // The following bits are added to packet marks for Tailscale use.
 //
-// We tried to pick bits sufficiently out of the way that it's
-// unlikely to collide with existing uses. We have 4 bytes of mark
-// bits to play with. We leave the lower byte alone on the assumption
-// that sysadmins would use those. Kubernetes uses a few bits in the
-// second byte, so we steer clear of that too.
+// The historical choice was to claim bits 16:23 (the third byte),
+// on the theory that documentation on packet marks online suggests
+// those marks are 16 bits wide and so the upper bytes are likely
+// unused in the wild. That theory does not hold on Android: netd
+// uses the entire low 21 bits for netId/permission/billing flags,
+// and the bytes immediately above that for vendor and "wakeup"
+// flags. The third byte (16:23) collides with the upper end of
+// netd's netId and the netd flag bits above it, which is what
+// causes Tailscale's bypass-mark rules to short-circuit netd's
+// per-network/per-uid policy.
 //
-// Empirically, most of the documentation on packet marks on the
-// internet gives the impression that the marks are 16 bits
-// wide. Based on this, we theorize that the upper two bytes are
-// relatively unused in the wild, and so we consume bits 16:23 (the
-// third byte).
+// To stay clear of all of the AOSP-defined fields, we live in the
+// upper half of netd's "reserved" byte (bits 25:28). That leaves
+// bits 21:24 free for vendor/AOSP growth and rooted-user
+// customization, and avoids bits 29:31 which AOSP uses for vendor
+// and wakeup flags. The mask covers the four reserved-upper-half
+// bits; only two of them are currently assigned.
 //
 // The constants are in the iptables/iproute2 string format for
 // matching and setting the bits, so they can be directly embedded in
 // commands.
 const (
 	// The mask for reading/writing the 'firewall mask' bits on a packet.
-	// See the comment on the const block on why we only use the third byte.
-	//
-	// We claim bits 16:23 entirely. For now we only use the lower four
-	// bits, leaving the higher 4 bits for future use.
-	LinuxFwmarkMask    = "0xff0000"
-	LinuxFwmarkMaskNum = 0xff0000
+	LinuxFwmarkMask    = "0x1e000000"
+	LinuxFwmarkMaskNum = 0x1e000000
 
 	// Packet is from Tailscale and to a subnet route destination, so
 	// is allowed to be routed through this machine.
-	LinuxSubnetRouteMark    = "0x40000"
-	LinuxSubnetRouteMarkNum = 0x40000
+	LinuxSubnetRouteMark    = "0x08000000"
+	LinuxSubnetRouteMarkNum = 0x08000000
 
 	// Packet was originated by tailscaled itself, and must not be
 	// routed over the Tailscale network.
-	LinuxBypassMark    = "0x80000"
-	LinuxBypassMarkNum = 0x80000
+	LinuxBypassMark    = "0x10000000"
+	LinuxBypassMarkNum = 0x10000000
 )
diff --git a/util/linuxfw/iptables_runner.go b/util/linuxfw/iptables_runner.go
index ad983fb..5eeff57 100644
--- a/util/linuxfw/iptables_runner.go
+++ b/util/linuxfw/iptables_runner.go
@@ -548,7 +548,7 @@ func (i *iptablesRunner) AddConnmarkSaveRule() error {
 		for _, rule := range rules {
 			if strings.Contains(rule, "CONNMARK") &&
 				strings.Contains(rule, "restore-mark") &&
-				strings.Contains(rule, "ctmask 0xff0000") {
+				strings.Contains(rule, "ctmask "+fwmarkMask) {
 				// Rules already exist, skip adding
 				return nil
 			}
diff --git a/util/linuxfw/nftables_runner.go b/util/linuxfw/nftables_runner.go
index dd09618..7f4699d 100644
--- a/util/linuxfw/nftables_runner.go
+++ b/util/linuxfw/nftables_runner.go
@@ -2128,6 +2128,9 @@ func (n *nftablesRunner) DelStatefulRule(tunname string) error {
 // makeConnmarkRestoreExprs creates nftables expressions to restore mark from conntrack.
 // Implements: ct state established,related ct mark & 0xff0000 != 0 meta mark set ct mark & 0xff0000
 //
+// NOTE: 0xff0000 in the comments here and below is upstream's stock
+// mask; the code actually uses tsconst.LinuxFwmarkMask (0x1e000000).
+//
 // LIMITATION: Unlike iptables CONNMARK --restore-mark with --nfmask, this implementation
 // overwrites non-Tailscale bits in the packet mark rather than merging them. This is a
 // fundamental limitation of the Linux kernel's nftables expression VM (not the Go library).
